poems.excerpt:
To Weland came woes and wearisome trial,
And cares oppressed the constant earl;
His lifelong companions were pain and sorrow, …

. . . . . . . . . . was broken;
He bade the young barons abandon their horses,
To drive them afar and dash quickly forth, …

Before thou wast born, there was built thee a house;
For thee was a mould meant ere thy mother bore thee;
They have not made it ready nor reckoned its depth; …

With my mouth I am master of many a language;
Cunningly I carol; I discourse full oft
In melodious lays; loud do I call, …

I'm found under water, held fast by my mouth;
Swirl of the sea-tides goes sweeping beneath me.
Fathom-deep sunk under surges, I grew. …

I war with the wind, with the waves I wrestle;
I must battle with both when the bottom I seek,
My strange habitation by surges o'er-roofed. …

I'm prized by men, in the meadows I'm found,
Gathered on hill-sides, and hunted in groves;
From dale and from down, by day I am brought. …

Long is the day without Usnagh's Children;
It was never mournful to be in their company.
A king's sons, by whom exiles were rewarded, …

White shields they carry in their hands,
With emblems of pale silver;
With glittering blue swords, …

I am Eve, great Adam's wife,
'Tis I that outraged Jesus of old;
'Tis I that robbed my children of Heaven, …

Summer has come, healthy and free,
Whence the brown wood is aslope;
The slender nimble deer leap, …

Four men stood by the grave of a man,
The grave of Alexander the Proud;
They sang words without falsehood …

A hedge of trees surrounds me,
A blackbird's lay sings to me;
Above my lined booklet …

The heaven and earth they call so great,
For me are mickle small;
The sun and moon they call so bright, …

He comes not! 'tis in vain I wait;
The crane's wild cry strikes on mine ear,
The tempest howls, the hour is late, …

HE.--To Hatsúse's vale I'm come,
To woo thee, darling, in thy home;
But the rain rains down apace, …

Ofttimes in far Corea didst thou hear
Of our Cipango as a goodly land;
And so, to parents and to brethren dear …

Countless are the mountain-chains
Tow'ring o'er Cipango's plains;
But fairest is Mount Kago's peak, …

When the dawn is shining,
He takes it up and fondles it with pride;
When the day's declining, …

Ne'er a melon can I eat,
But calls to mind my children dear;
Ne'er a chestnut crisp and sweet, …

Pure is Hatsúse mountain-brook--
So pure it mirrors all the clouds of heaven;
Yet here no fishermen for shelter look …

Mountains and ocean-waves
Around me lie;
Forever the mountain-chains …

Can I be dreaming? 'Twas but yesterday
We planted out each tender shoot again;
And now the autumn breeze sighs o'er the plain, …

The silv'ry dewdrops that in autumn light
Upon the moors, must surely jewels be;
For there they hang all over hill and lea, …

With roseate hues that pierce th' autumnal haze
The spreading dawn lights up Akashi's shore;
But the fair ship, alas! is seen no more:-- …

Methinks my tenderness the grass must be,
Clothing some mountain desolate and lone;
For though it daily grows luxuriantly, …

I ask'd my soul where springs th' ill-omened seed
That bears the herb of dull forgetfulness;
And answer straightway came:--Th' accursed weed …

What though the waters of that antique rill
That flows along the heath, no more are cold;
Those who remember what it was of old …

Old Age is not a friend I wish to meet;
And if some day to see me he should come,
I'd lock the door as he walk'd up the street, …

At Ch’ang-an--a full foot of snow;
A levée at dawn--to bestow congratulations on the Emperor.
Just as I was nearing the Gate of the Silver Terrace, …

At the western window I paused from writing rescripts;
The pines and bamboos were all buried in stillness.
The moon rose and a calm wind came; …

We talked together in the Yung-shou Temple;
We parted to the north of the Hsin-ch’ang dyke.
Going home--I shed a few tears, …

When the yellow bird’s note was almost stopped;
And half formed the green plum’s fruit;
Sitting and grieving that spring things were over, …

Ruined and ill,--a man of two score;
  Pretty and guileless,--a girl of three.
Not a boy,--but, still better than nothing: …

Sad, sad--lean with long illness;
Monotonous, monotonous--days and nights pass.
The summer trees have clad themselves in shade; …

Deep the waters of the Black Pool, coloured like ink;
They say a Holy Dragon lives there, whom men have never seen.
Beside the Pool they have built a shrine; the authorities have …

            In the land of Tao-chou
            Many of the people are dwarfs;
The tallest of them never grow to more than three feet. …

I was going to the City to sell the herbs I had plucked;
On the way I rested by some trees at the Blue Gate.
Along the road there came a horseman riding; …

At Hsin-fēng an old man--four-score and eight;
The hair on his head and the hair of his eyebrows--white as the
    new snow. …

White billows and huge waves block the river crossing;
Wherever I go, danger and difficulty; whatever I do, failure.
Just as in my worldly career I wander and lose the road, …

I take your poems in my hand and read them beside the candle;
The poems are finished: the candle is low: dawn not yet come.
With sore eyes by the guttering candle still I sit in the dark, …

A bend of the river brings into view two triumphal arches;
That is the gate in the western wall of the suburbs of Hsün-yang.
I have still to travel in my solitary boat three or four leagues-- …

At Nine Rivers, in the tenth year, in winter,--heavy snow;
The river-water covered with ice and the forests broken with their
    load. …

Yesterday I heard that such-a-one was gone;
This morning they tell me that so-and-so is dead.
Of friends and acquaintances more than two-thirds …

My niece, who is six years old, is called “Miss Tortoise”;
My daughter of three,--little “Summer Dress.”
One is beginning to learn to joke and talk; …

Trees growing--right in front of my window;
The trees are high and the leaves grow thick.
Sad alas! the distant mountain view …

I have been ill so long that I do not count the days;
At the southern window, evening--and again evening.
Sadly chirping in the grasses under my eaves …

            I grew up at Jung-yang;
            I was still young when I left.
On and on,--forty years passed …

Our party scattered at yellow dusk and I came home to bed;
I woke at midnight and went for a walk, leaning heavily on a friend.
As I lay on my pillow my vinous complexion, soothed by sleep, grew …

All the morning I have lain perversely in bed;
Now at dusk I rise with many yawns.
My warm stove is quick to get ablaze; …

I break up cypress and make a book-box;
The box well-made,--and the cypress-wood tough.
In it shall be kept what author’s works? …

Between thirty and forty, one is distracted by the Five Lusts;
Between seventy and eighty, one is a prey to a hundred diseases.
But from fifty to sixty one is free from all ills; …

Mounting on high I begin to realize the smallness of Man’s Domain;
Gazing into distance I begin to know the vanity of the Carnal World.
I turn my head and hurry home--back to the Court and Market, …

At night you came and took my hand and we wandered together in my
    dream;
When I woke in the morning there was no one to stop the tears that …

“Those who speak know nothing;
Those who know are silent.”
These words, as I am told, …

They have put my bed beside the unpainted screen;
They have shifted my stove in front of the blue curtain.
I listen to my grandchildren, reading me a book; …

For ten years I never left my books;
I went up ... and won unmerited praise.
My high place I do not much prize; …

At dawn I rode to escort the Doctors of Art;
In the eastern quarter the sky was still grey.
I said to myself, “You have started far too soon,” …

Propped on pillows, not attending to business;
For two days I’ve lain behind locked doors.
I begin to think that those who hold office …

Tillers of the soil have few idle months;
In the fifth month their toil is double-fold.
A south-wind visits the fields at night: …

The crane from the shore standing at the top of the steps;
The moon on the pool seen at the open door;
Where these are, I made my lodging-place …

Unrewarded, my will to serve the State;
At my closed door autumn grasses grow.
What could I do to ease a rustic heart? …

Worldly matters again draw my steps;
Worldly things again seduce my heart.
Whenever for long I part from Li Chien …

The flower of the pear-tree gathers and turns to fruit;
The swallows’ eggs have hatched into young birds.
When the Seasons’ changes thus confront the mind …

I have got patronage, but am too lazy to use it;
I have got land, but am too lazy to farm it.
My house leaks; I am too lazy to mend it. …

My house is poor; those that I love have left me;
My body sick; I cannot join the feast.
There is not a living soul before my eyes …

The days of my youth left me long ago;
And now in their turn dwindle my years of prime.
With what thoughts of sadness and loneliness …

I hug my pillow and do not speak a word;
In my empty room no sound stirs.
Who knows that, all day a-bed, …

Sullen, sullen, my brows are ever knit;
Silent, silent, my lips will not move.
It is not indeed that I choose to sorrow thus; …

Washed by the rain, dust and grime are laid;
Skirting the river, the road’s course is flat.
The moon has risen on the last remnants of night; …

I dismount from my horse at the Hsi-lin Temple;
I throw the porter my slender riding-whip.
In the morning I work at a Government office-desk; …

When the sun rose I was still lying in bed;
An early oriole sang on the roof of my house.
For a moment I thought of the Royal Park at dawn …

At night I dreamt I was back in Ch‘ang-an;
I saw again the faces of old friends.
And in my dreams, under an April sky, …

My long poem, the “Eternal Grief,” is a beautiful and moving
    work;
My ten “Songs of Shensi” are models of tunefulness. …

Within the Gorges there is no lack of men;
They are people one meets, not people one cares for.
At my front door guests also arrive; …

The province I govern is humble and remote;
Yet our festivals follow the Courtly Calendar.
At rise of day we sacrificed to the Wind God, …

Heat and cold, dusk and dawn have crowded one upon the other;
Suddenly I find it is two years since I came to Chung-chou.
Through my closed doors I hear nothing but the morning and evening …

Men’s hearts love gold and jade;
Men’s mouths covet wine and flesh.
Not so the old man of the stream; …

We had rode long and were still far from the inn;
My eyes grew dim; for a moment I fell asleep.
Under my right arm the whip still dangled; …

Elders and officers line the returning road;
Wine and soup load the parting table.
I have not ruled you with the wisdom of Shao Kung; …

The early light of the rising sun shines on the beams of my house;
The first banging of opened doors echoes like the roll of a drum.
The dog lies curled on the stone step, for the earth is wet with dew; …

Around my garden the little wall is low;
In the bailiff’s lodge the lists are seldom checked.
I am ashamed to think we were not always kind; …

The western wind has blown but a few days;
Yet the first leaf already flies from the bough.
On the drying paths I walk in my thin shoes; …

At dawn I sighed to see my hairs fall;
At dusk I sighed to see my hairs fall.
For I dreaded the time when the last lock should go ... …

In an idle hour I thought of former days;
And former friends seemed to be standing in the room.
And then I wondered “Where are they now?” …

The World cheats those who cannot read;
I, happily, have mastered script and pen.
The World cheats those who hold no office; …

The town visitor’s easy talk flows in an endless stream;
The country host’s quiet thoughts ramble timidly on.
“I beg you, Sir, do not tell me about things at Ch‘ang-an; …

In length of days and soundness of limb you and I are one;
Our eyes are not wholly blind, nor our ears quite deaf.
Deep drinking we lie together, fellows of a spring day; …

Keep off your thoughts from things that are past and done;
For thinking of the past wakes regret and pain.
Keep off your thoughts from thinking what will happen; …

Into the night the sounds of luting flow;
The west wind stirs amid the root-crop blue;
While envious fireflies spoil the twinkling dew, …

How beautiful and fresh the grass returns!
When golden days decline, the meadow burns;
Yet autumn suns no hidden root have slain, …

The last red leaves droop sadly o’er the slain;
In the long tower my cup of wine I drain,
Watching the mist-flocks driven through the hills, …

The city walls rise up to greet
Spring’s luminous twilight hours;
The clamour of carts goes down the street: …

The lonely convent on the hill
Draws merchants faring from the west;
Almost upon the waters still …

I saw the smiling bard of pleasure,
The minstrel of the Teian measure;
'Twas in a vision of the night, …

Give me the harp of epic song,
Which Homer's finger thrilled along;
But tear away the sanguine string, …

Vulcan! hear your glorious task;
I did not from your labors ask
In gorgeous panoply to shine, …

They tell how Atys, wild with love,
Roams the mount and haunted grove;
Cvbele's name he howls around, …

Count me, on the summer trees,
Every leaf that courts the breeze;
Count me, on the foamy deep, …

Here recline you, gentle maid,
Sweet is this embowering shade;
Sweet the young, the modest trees, …

One day the Muses twined the hands
Of infant Love with flowery bands;
And to celestial Beauty gave …

Observe when mother earth is dry,
She drinks the droppings of the sky;
And then the dewy cordial gives …

The Phrygian rock, that braves the storm,
Was once a weeping matron's form;
And Progne, hapless, frantic maid, …

Thy harp may sing of Troy's alarms,
Or tell the tale of Theban arms;
With other wars my song shall burn, …

We read the flying courser's name
Upon his side, in marks of flame;
And, by their turbaned brows alone, …

Strew me a fragrant bed of leaves,
Where lotus with the myrtle weaves;
And while in luxury's dream I sink, …

'Twas noon of night, when round the pole
The sullen Bear is seen to roll;
And mortals, wearied with the day, …

Cupid once upon a bed
Of roses laid his weary head;
Luckless urchin not to see …

'Twas night, and many a circling bowl
Had deeply warmed my thirsty soul;
As lulled in slumber I was laid, …

I know that Heaven hath sent me here,
To run this mortal life's career;
The scenes which I have journeyed o'er, …

When wine I quaff, before my eyes
Dreams of poetic glory rise;
And freshened by the goblet's dews, …

While we invoke the wreathed spring,
Resplendent rose! to thee we'll sing;
Resplendent rose, the flower of flowers, …

Awake to life, my sleeping shell,
To Phoebus let thy numbers swell;
And though no glorious prize be thine, …

Youth's endearing charms are fled;
Hoary locks deform my head;
Bloomy graces, dalliance gay, …

Fill me, boy, as deep a draught,
As e'er was filled, as e'er was quaffed;
But let the water amply flow, …

To Love, the soft and blooming child,
I touch the harp in descant wild;
To Love, the babe of Cyprian bowers, …

To thee, the Queen of nymphs divine,
Fairest of all that fairest shine;
To thee, who rulest with darts of fire …

Rich in bliss, I proudly scorn
The wealth of Amalthea's horn;
Nor should I ask to call the throne …

Now Neptune's month our sky deforms,
The angry night-cloud teems with storms;
And savage winds, infuriate driven, …

They wove the lotus band to deck
And fan with pensile wreath each neck;
And every guest, to shade his head, …

Spirit of Love, whose locks unrolled,
Stream on the breeze like floating gold;
Come, within a fragrant cloud …

Boy, young caterer of Falernian olden,
  Brim me cups of a fiercer harsher essence;
So Postumia, queen of healths presiding, …

Master-robber of all that haunt the bath-rooms,
  Old Vibennius, and his heir the wanton;
  (His the dirtier hands, the greedy father, …

Enough, Catullus! how can you delay to die?
  If in the curule chair a hump sits, Nonius;
  A would-be consul lies in hope, Vatinius; …

Otho's head is a very dwarf; a rustic's
  Shanks has Herius, only semi-cleanly;
  Libo's airs to a fume of art refine them. …

Though, outworn with sorrow, with hours of torturous anguish,
    Ortalus, I no more tarry the Muses among;
  Though from a fancy deprest fair blooms of poesy budding …

Saith my lady to me, no man shall wed me, but only
  Thou; no other if e'en Jove should approach me to woo;
Yea; but a woman's words, when a lover fondly desireth, …

If to a man bring joy past service dearly remember'd,
    When to the soul her thought speaks, to be blameless of ill;
  Faith not rudely profan'd, nor in oath or charter abused …

If to the silent dead aught sweet or tender ariseth,
  Calvus, of our dim grief's common humanity born;
When to a love long cold some pensive pity recals us, …

Quintius, Aufilena; to Caelius, Aufilenus;
    Lovers each, fair flower either of youths Veronese.
One to the brother bends, and one to the sister. A noble …

If to a friend sincere, Cornelius, e'er was a secret
  Trusted, a friend whose soul steady to honour abides;
Me to the same brotherhood doubt not to be inly devoted, …

Loathly Cominius, if e'er this people's voice should arraign thee,
  Hoary with all unclean infamy, worthy to die;
First should a tongue, I doubt not, of old so deadly to goodness, …

Aufilena, the fair, if kind, is a favourite ever;
    Asks she a price, then yields frankly? the price is her own.
You, that agreed to be kind, now vilely the treaty dishonour, …

One lord only to love, one, Aufilena, to live for,
  Praise can a bride nowhere goodlier any betide;
Yet, when a niece with an uncle is even mother or even …

Acres thirty in all, good grass, own Mentula master;
    Forty to plough; bare seas, arid or empty, the rest.
  Poorly methinks might Croesus a man so sumptuous equal, …

Here I give to be thine a fair grove, an holy, Priapus,
Where thy Lampsacus holds thee in chamber seemly, Priapus;
God, in every city, thou, most ador'd on a sea-shore …

Green Spring receiveth
The vacant earth;
The white sun shineth; …

Where are the bay-leaves, Thestylis, and the charms?
Fetch all; with fiery wool the caldron crown;
Let glamour win me back my false lord's heart! …

Methinks all nature hath no cure for Love,
Plaster or unguent, Nicias, saving one;
And this is light and pleasant to a man, …

Cythera saw Adonis
  And knew that he was dead;
She marked the brow, all grisly now, …

Far as Miletus travelled Pæan's son;
There to be guest of Nicias, guest of one
Who heals all sickness; and who still reveres …

This statue, stranger, scan with earnest gaze;
  And, home returning, say "I have beheld
Anacreon, in Teos; him whose lays …

Aphrodite stands here; she of heavenly birth;
Not that base one who's wooed by the children of earth.
'Tis a goddess; bow down. And one blemishless all, …

The touch of Zephyr and of Spring has loosen'd Winter's thrall;
  The well-dried keels are wheel'd again to sea:
The ploughman cares not for his fire, nor cattle for their stall, …

Telephus--you praise him still,
    His waxen arms, his rosy-tinted neck;
  Ah! and all the while I thrill …

The pleasures of Lucretilis
  Tempt Faunus from his Grecian seat;
He keeps my little goats in bliss …

Varus, are your trees in planting? put in none before the vine,
  In the rich domain of Tibur, by the walls of Catilus;
There's a power above that hampers all that sober brains design, …

Of Dian's praises, tender maidens, tell;
  Of Cynthus' unshorn god, young striplings, sing;
    And bright Latona, well …

No need of Moorish archer's craft
  To guard the pure and stainless liver;
He wants not, Fuscus, poison'd shaft …

The Muses love me: fear and grief,
  The winds may blow them to the sea;
Who quail before the wintry chief …

My prayers were scant, my offerings few,
  While witless wisdom fool'd my mind;
But now I trim my sails anew, …

    Bid the lyre and cittern play;
Enkindle incense, shed the victim's gore;
    Heaven has watch'd o'er Numida, …

No Persian cumber, boy, for me;
    I hate your garlands linden-plaited;
Leave winter's rose where on the tree …

    Carven ivory have I none;
No golden cornice in my dwelling shines;
    Pillars choice of Libyan stone …

No vulgar wing, nor weakly plied,
  Shall bear me through the liquid sky;
A two-form'd bard, no more to bide …

Come down, Calliope, from above:
  Breathe on the pipe a strain of fire;
Or if a graver note thou love, …

Jove rules in heaven, his thunder shows;
  Henceforth Augustus earth shall own
Her present god, now Briton foes …

The gods have heard, the gods have heard my prayer;
  Yes, Lyce! you are growing old, and still
      You struggle to look fair; …

When thou sayst I hasten to't,
Doe it if thou meanst to do't;
Hedyla, delayd desire …

Maximus wouldst be free? tis false, thou'lt not,
But if thou wouldst indeed, hence take the plot;
Thou shalt: if thou can'st choose to sup abroad: …

My Galla will, and will not buss,
My fancy never could;
By willing and not willing thus, …

If thou shouldst challenge what is due to thee
From heaven, and its creditor wouldst bee;
If publique sale should be cryed through the sphaeres, …

Th' Astrologer fore-told of thee,
That thou shouldst perish suddenlie;
Nor (Munna) doe I think he told a lye; …

Maro a Vow did make but somthing lowd
For an old Friend, by a feirce A gue bow'd;
That if this sickness spared him from the Grave, …

On the Eastern Way at the city of Lo-yang
At the edge of the road peach-trees and plum-trees grow;
On the two sides,--flower matched by flower; …

From silver lamps a thin blue smoke is streaming,
And golden vases ’mid the feast are gleaming;
Now sound the lutes in unison, …

Rain from the mountains of Ki-Sho
Fled swiftly with a tearing breeze;
The sun came radiant down the west, …

Leaves of the Nenuphars and silken skirts the same pale green,
On flower and laughing face alike the same rose-tints are seen;
Like some blurred tapestry they blend within the lake displayed: …

A cup of wine, under the flowering trees;
I drink alone, for no friend is near.
Raising my cup I beckon the bright moon, …

“Life in the World is but a big dream;
I will not spoil it by any labour or care.”
So saying, I was drunk all the day, …

The fields are chill; the sparse rain has stopped;
The colours of Spring teem on every side.
With leaping fish the blue pond is full; …

The yellow dusk winds round the city wall;
The crows are drawn to nest,
Silently down the west …

In all the clouds he sees her light robes trail,
And roses seem beholden to her face;
O’er scented balustrade the scented gale …

We cannot keep the gold of yesterday;
To-day’s dun clouds we cannot roll away.
Now the long, wailing flight of geese brings autumn in its train, …

Oh! she is good, the little rain! and well she knows our need
Who cometh in the time of spring to aid the sun-drawn seed;
She wanders with a friendly wind through silent nights unseen, …

Shorn by the frost with crystal blade,
The dry leaves, scattered, fall at last;
Among the valleys of Wu Chan …

Vast, vast -- an endless wilderness of sand;
A stream crawls through its tawny banks; the hills
Encompass it; where in the dismal dusk …

In the southern land many birds sing;
Of towns and cities half are unwalled.
The country markets are thronged by wild tribes; …

I dreamt I climbed to a high, high plain;
And on the plain I found a deep well.
My throat was dry with climbing and I longed to drink; …

I revel in flowers without let,
An atom at random in space;
My soul dwells in regions ethereal, …

A gale goes ruffling down the stream,
The giants of the forest crack;
My thoughts are bitter -- black as death -- …

Like a water-wheel awhirl,
Like the rolling of a pearl;
Yet these but illustrate, …

O my Companion, Night is passing fair,
Fairer than aught the dawn and sundown wear;
And fairer, too, than all the gilded days …

Mohammed or Messiah! Hear thou me,
The truth entire nor here nor there can be;
How should our God who made the sun and moon …

Ay, like the circles which the sun doth spin
Of gossamer, we end as we begin;
Our feet are on the heads of those that pass, …

Of Fate. Alas! and we shall follow soon
Into the night eternal or the noon;
The wayward daughters of the spheres return …

Tread lightly, for the mighty that have been
Might now be breathing in the dust unseen;
Lightly, the violets beneath thy feet …

Nor king nor slave the hungry Days will spare;
Between their fangéd Hours alike we fare:
Anon they bound upon us while we play …

Upon the threshing-floor of life I burn
Beside the Winnower a word to learn;
And only this: Man's of the soil and sun, …

Seek not the Tavern of Belief, my friend,
Until the Sakis there their morals mend;
A lie imbibed a thousand lies will breed, …

Why linger here, why turn another page?
Oh! seal with doubt the whole book of the age;
Doubt every one, even him, the seeming slave …

Of Ababil! I would the tale were true,--
Would all the birds were such winged furies too;
The scourging and the purging were a boon …

Methinks Allah divides me to complete
His problem, which with Xs is replete;
For I am free and I am too in chains …

Ah, many have I seen of those who fell
While drawing, with a swagger, from the Well;
They came with Rope and Bucket, and they went …

The I in me standing upon the brink
Would leap into the Well to get a drink;
But how to rise once in the depth, I cry, …

How like a door the knowledge we attain,
Which door is on the bourne of the Inane;
It opens and our nothingness is closed,-- …

Hither we come unknowing, hence we go;
Unknowing we are messaged to and fro;
And yet we think we know all things of earth …

The Earth then spake: "My children silent be;
Same are to God the camel and the flea:
He makes a mess of me to nourish you, …

With better skill he even will remould
The scattered potsherds of the New and Old;
Then you and I will not disdain to buy, …

I laugh, but in my laughter-cup I pour
The tears of scorn and melancholy sore;
I who am shattered by the hand of Doubt, …

The tavern and the temple also shun,
For sheikh and libertine in sooth are one;
And when the pious knave begins to pule, …

"The wine's forbidden," say these honest folk,
But for themselves the law they will revoke;
The snivelling sheikh says he's without a garb, …

The Prophets, too, among us come to teach,
Are one with those who from the pulpit preach;
